 Let's break this down:
 overwhelm Tiffany baked 8 brownies, and she needs 17 in total for her party. To find out how many more brownies she needs to bake, we subtract the number she's baked from the number she needs:

\boxed{\text{Extra brownies needed}=17-8=\boxed{9}}

Tiffany used 8 cups of flour per brownie, so to find out how many cups of flour she has already used, we multiply the number of brownies she's baked by the amount of flour used per brownie:

\boxed{\text{Flour used}=8\times 8=\boxed{64}}

Now we find out how many cups of flour she still needs by subtracting the amount of flour she has already used from the amount she needs per brownie multiplied by the number of extra brownies needed:

\boxed{\text{Flour still needed} = (8+\text{extra brownies needed})\times 8 -\text{Flour used}}

\boxed{\text{Flour still needed} = (8+9)\times 8 -64}

\boxed{\text{Flour still needed} = 17\times 8 -64}

\boxed{\text{Flour still needed} = 136-64 = \boxed{72}}

So Tiffany still needs \boxed{72} cups of flour for the remaining brownies she plans to bake.

